93-
94-- **Not normal.** Shapiro-Wilk rejects normality for every case (p < 0.001), on t and on
95-  log t. Every distribution is right-skewed (skew 1.1 to 3.2, and 16.6 for one case with a
96-  single 5× outlier): a sharp edge near the fastest call and a tail of slower ones, the shape
97-  of interference that can only add time. A log-normal fits better than a normal in every
98-  case, but none is log-normal either.
99-- **Scheduling moves the mean by a few percent.** The mean is at most 4.2 % above the median,
100-  and the outliers (calls slower than median + 3 robust sigmas) raise the mean by at most
101-  3.8 % (*tail excess*). Mean and median give the same speedups to within a few percent.
102-  Because the distributions are skewed, ± stddev overstates the spread below the mean; the
103-  p5 – p95 column shows the real range.
104-- **Slow calls come in runs.** Lag-1 autocorrelation is 0.3 to 0.7, so neighbouring calls are
105-  alike. Isolated preemptions would hit calls independently; runs point to slower changes in
106-  machine state (CPU frequency, caches, the core the process runs on). It is clearest for
107-  musicrs IFB-MUSIC with one bin on the 1 s recording: two clusters, ~650 µs and ~790 µs. A
108-  re-run reproduced both clusters, in a different order.
109-- **Run to run**, a second musicrs run gave means 3 to 8 % different. Read the numbers as
110-  about ± 10 %; the speedups are far larger than that.
